{
  "_label": "FAKE keys -- generated for these vectors (OpenSSL 3.6.4, prime256v1), never registered anywhere. The private halves were not kept in this repository.",
  "_what": [
    "Known-answer vectors for the native app's signed requests (ADR 0045 §2.3, M16-05). internal/appdevice's tests read this file; the iOS and Android request builders (M16-12, M16-16) are to read it too, so the three implementations are held to ONE byte string.",
    "canonical_text is the exact signed byte string: six lines, each ended by LF -- the prefix tappa-app-request-v1, the method, the path, the lower-case hex SHA-256 of the raw body bytes, the nonce as issued, the device id in canonical lower-case form.",
    "Signature: ECDSA P-256 with SHA-256 over canonical_text (the signer hashes it: iOS .ecdsaSignatureMessageX962SHA256, Android SHA256withECDSA), ASN.1 DER, sent as unpadded base64url in the App-Signature header. The nonce goes in App-Nonce, the device id in App-Device-Id.",
    "public_point_hex is a device's public key in the stored form: the raw uncompressed point 04 || X || Y, 65 bytes (00108). It is named for the point so that redline R7d's key-context rule (that word plus a long hex string on one line) is not tripped by a PUBLIC value: the rule stays blunt and the name is reworded, this repository's direction.",
    "reads / read_signatures (M16-07): the SIGNED READ's canonical text -- GET /app/v1/me, POST /app/v1/tap/start, POST /app/v1/nonce carry a signed timestamp instead of a nonce. Six lines, each ended by LF: the prefix tappa-app-read-v1, the method, the path, the hex SHA-256 of the raw body, the decimal Unix second (no sign, no leading zero) as sent in App-Timestamp, the device id. The server accepts it within 60 s of its own clock either way. Key C is FAKE like A and B; its private half was not kept."
  ],
  "_provenance": [
    "Every canonical_text was written with printf and hashed with shasum, independently of the Go builder; internal/appdevice's test asserts its builder reproduces each one byte for byte.",
    "Every signature was made by OpenSSL (openssl dgst -sha256 -sign), NOT by Go, and every 'valid: true' one was verified by OpenSSL as well -- so the verifier is checked against a second implementation, not against its own output. tap-low-s-twin is tap-high-s with s replaced by n-s (computed outside Go), and OpenSSL verifies it.",
    "LIMIT: the canonical format itself is this repository's definition; the vectors prove the implementations agree with it, not that the format is right.",
    "M16-07: key C was generated by OpenSSL 3.6.4 (ecparam prime256v1); the read texts were written with printf and hashed with shasum; every read signature was made by openssl dgst -sha256 -sign and the three valid ones verified by openssl dgst -verify (and the swapped ones checked to FAIL there, plus a one-second-tampered timestamp). Not by Go."
  ],
